Render the Linux web view on a Mesa GPU when NVIDIA's proprietary driver would
be chosen.

WebKitGTK 2.52 cannot allocate its GBM buffers on NVIDIA's proprietary driver
("Failed to create GBM buffer: Invalid argument") and silently falls back to
non-composited software mode, which repaints the whole page on one CPU thread
every frame. That fallback happens patch or no patch, which is why
juce-linux-webkit-gpu.patch on its own changed nothing on such a machine. Stock
MiniBrowser behaves the same way, natively on Wayland too.

A machine with a second, Mesa-driven GPU (typically an iGPU beside the NVIDIA
card) composites fine on that one, and WebKit can be told to use it through
WEBKIT_WEB_RENDER_DEVICE_FILE. Measured on an RTX 3060 beside a Radeon iGPU, a
CSS animation's web process CPU drops from ~90% to ~4% at a scale factor of 2.

So, in the forked child, before GTK starts: if some render node is driven by
"nvidia" and another accessible one is not, point WebKit at the first such
node. A device the user named is left alone, and a machine with no NVIDIA node
or no alternative is untouched. Doing it in the child keeps the setenv
single-threaded and out of the host's environment, which matters inside a DAW,
and every WebKit process the child launches inherits it.

diff --git a/modules/juce_gui_extra/native/juce_WebBrowserComponent_linux.cpp b/modules/juce_gui_extra/native/juce_WebBrowserComponent_linux.cpp
--- a/modules/juce_gui_extra/native/juce_WebBrowserComponent_linux.cpp
+++ b/modules/juce_gui_extra/native/juce_WebBrowserComponent_linux.cpp
@@ -767,6 +767,41 @@ struct ResourceRequestResponse
     static inline const String key { "resourceRequestResponse" };
 };
 
+//==============================================================================
+// PLECTRIFY_WEBKIT_RENDER_DEVICE: keep WebKit off NVIDIA's proprietary driver
+// when the machine has another GPU to render on. WebKitGTK cannot allocate its
+// GBM buffers there and silently falls back to repainting the whole page on one
+// CPU thread, so the GPU policy above buys nothing; a Mesa render node (an
+// iGPU beside the NVIDIA card) composites properly. Runs in the freshly
+// exec'd child before GTK starts, so the setenv is single-threaded and never
+// touches the host's environment, and every WebKit process inherits it. A
+// device the user named is left alone.
+static void plectrifyAvoidNvidiaRenderDevice()
+{
+    if (std::getenv ("WEBKIT_WEB_RENDER_DEVICE_FILE") != nullptr)
+        return;
+
+    auto nodes = File ("/sys/class/drm").findChildFiles (File::findDirectories, false, "renderD*");
+    nodes.sort();
+
+    auto sawNvidia = false;
+    String alternative;
+
+    for (const auto& node : nodes)
+    {
+        const auto driver = node.getChildFile ("device/driver").getLinkedTarget().getFileName();
+        const auto device = "/dev/dri/" + node.getFileName();
+
+        if (driver == "nvidia")
+            sawNvidia = true;
+        else if (driver.isNotEmpty() && alternative.isEmpty() && access (device.toRawUTF8(), R_OK | W_OK) == 0)
+            alternative = device;
+    }
+
+    if (sawNvidia && alternative.isNotEmpty())
+        setenv ("WEBKIT_WEB_RENDER_DEVICE_FILE", alternative.toRawUTF8(), 0);
+}
+
 //==============================================================================
 class GtkChildProcess final : private CommandReceiver::Responder
 {
@@ -796,6 +831,8 @@ public:
             }
         }
 
+        plectrifyAvoidNvidiaRenderDevice();
+
         auto& wk = *WebKitSymbols::getInstance();
 
         // webkit2gtk crashes when using the wayland backend embedded into an x11 window
